{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794405531,"nonce":"mtuVcQagJ6ObjXyEvwFGRWW9uLI=","title":"The step solve of an error-controlled march ignores the march's rtol and warm-starts far from the answer","message":"Every step solve runs Jacobi BiCGStab to a fixed tol=1e-10, whatever .adaptive(rtol=) asks, and starts from the shifted state psi the BDF formula forms, not from the predictor P(t_{n+1}). Measured with a counting linear= slot on a 2-D P1 heat march, bdf().adaptive(rtol=1e-6): the warm start's relative residual is ~1e-2 per solve, where the predictor sits at the local-error level. Loosening the tolerance has a floor: PCG at tol=1e-8 cost about a quarter fewer matvecs than at 1e-10 and moved the final error a little; at tol=1e-6 the polluted error estimates rejected steps until the attempt budget ran out (NaN). Direction: the predictor as x0, and a solve tolerance tied to rtol well below it (Newton-type schemes use a fraction of the step tolerance). Probe: probes/transient_step_krylov.py.","files":null}]}